# The build context for `storefront/Dockerfile`, which is the repository
# root.
#
# This repository's builders -- verified against podman 5.4.2 / buildah
# 1.39.3 only, and T12 will exercise a different one, which is the builder
# this claim needs to hold for -- read this file because it is named after
# the Dockerfile and sits beside it; a plain `storefront/.dockerignore` would
# be read only if the context were `storefront/`, which it cannot be -- the
# dependency tree comes from the workspace lockfile at the repository root.
# See `scripts/images.test.ts`.
#
# Bounded: these patterns reach the `build` stage of the Dockerfile only, and
# the runtime stage copies the traced standalone output selectively rather
# than the build context wholesale -- a decoy file that reaches the build
# context still cannot reach the image that ships unless something in the
# Dockerfile copies it in by name.

# Build output and dependency trees. Every one of these is produced inside the
# image from the lockfile; a copy from the developer's machine would silently
# replace it with whatever that machine happens to have -- and a stale
# `.next` copied into the context is the classic way a build-time value from
# somebody's workstation ends up in a published image.
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Version control, CI definitions and hooks. None of it is application code.
.git
.gitignore
.githooks
.github

# Tests and configuration that exist to run them.
**/tests
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test.tsx
**/vitest.config.ts
**/vitest.config.mts

# Design masters, in case one is ever committed by mistake -- this repository
# ships web derivatives only.
**/*.psd
**/*.ai
**/*.sketch
**/*.fig
**/*.xcf
**/*.afdesign
**/*.afphoto
**/*.tif
**/*.tiff

# Prose, plans and local environment files. Named patterns only -- `.env` and
# `.env.*`, not every shape a secret can take. A decoy like `env.local` or
# `id_rsa` reaches the build context; see the bounding note above for why
# that does not reach the image.
**/*.md
docs
**/.env
**/.env.*

# The other workspace. Its `package.json` is re-included because npm reads
# every workspace manifest before it resolves anything, even when installing
# only this one.
backend
!backend/package.json
